Subject: [PATCH 3/5] D14 amended: a shielded pool can enforce inside the proof
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The P0-A spike (privacy-lab, findings 001-010) ran Privacy Pools end to end on
Konstellation: Groth16 verifies, a full deposit -> withdraw cycle works with a
real proof, native KASH needs no WKASH wrap, and gas is 265k deposit / 465k
withdraw against a 10M block.

It also found D14's text misleading. D14 said the shielded interior is
unenforceable. That is true of the ante decorator and wrong about the system:
Privacy Pools requires every withdrawal to prove membership of the depositor in
an on-chain association set, inside the circuit. A deposit excluded from the
current set cannot produce a valid proof, so the funds cannot leave — enforcement
at the source, which no attacker can route around.

This is stronger than D14 assumed. Excluding a frozen depositor's label makes
those funds unwithdrawable regardless of recipient, narrowing the accepted leak
to the window before the next root is published.

The costs are recorded rather than glossed: a second highest-value key (the set's
publisher), curation as an ongoing staffed function, weaker privacy against the
set operator — who knows which deposits are approved, and whose root size bounds
the real anonymity set — and a liveness dependency, since withdrawals stop if
publication stops.

- ENGINEERING.md §11 D14: amendment with the ASP layer and its costs
- ENGINEERING.md §10.1: corrected the "interior unenforceable" framing
- ENGINEERING.md §10: association-set publisher added to the risk list beside the
  freeze authority, with a note that it must not be the same key
- STATUS.md §2, header: spike results and what remains open

Who holds that key is an open supervisor question, not settled here.

