 **D14 applies the same reasoning to privacy.** A shielded pool is entered and
 exited in the open, so the perimeter is already enforced and the interior is
-already invisible — gating the boundary is not a compromise reached under
-pressure, it is the only thing an ante decorator was ever able to do. The
-accepted leak: shield before a freeze, unshield to a fresh address after. Bounded
-to funds already shielded at freeze time. Recorded in D14, and it belongs in the
-whitepaper next to the risks above, not only in the code comments.
+already invisible to the decorator — gating the boundary is not a compromise
+reached under pressure, it is the only thing an ante decorator was ever able to do.
+
+**But "the decorator cannot reach it" is not the same as "nothing can."** The
+P0-A spike (2026-09-17) found that a shielded pool may enforce its own rules
+*inside the proof*. Privacy Pools requires every withdrawal to prove membership
+of the depositor in an association set published on chain; a deposit excluded
+from the current set cannot produce a valid proof, so the funds cannot leave.
+That is enforcement at the source rather than at the recipient, and it is not
+something an attacker can route around — the proof simply fails.
+
+This makes D14's accepted leak smaller than first stated. Excluding a frozen
+depositor's label makes those funds unwithdrawable regardless of recipient,
+narrowing the leak to the window between the freeze and the next root.
+
+It is not free, and the costs belong in the whitepaper beside the §10 risks
+above: a second highest-value key (whoever publishes the set), curation as an
+ongoing staffed function, weaker privacy against the set's operator — who
+necessarily knows which deposits are approved, and whose root size bounds the
+real anonymity set — and a liveness dependency, since withdrawals stop if
+publication stops.
+
+The residual leak, if no such layer exists or the set is not curated: shield
+before a freeze, unshield to a fresh address after, bounded to funds already
+shielded at freeze time.
 
